  "summary": "The ancient Japanese architectural style of Shinto shrines exemplified by Ise Shrine.",
  "description": "Characterized by simple, unpainted wood construction with raised floors and steep thatched roofs, symbolizing purity and renewal. Represents the oldest surviving Japanese architectural style, rebuilt every 20 years using traditional techniques without nails, embodying Shinto principles of impermanence and ritual purity.",
  "culturalContext": "Ise Jingu's shinden architecture preserves Japan's oldest shrine form through the Shikinen Sengu: every twenty years since 690 CE the entire shrine — inner sanctum, treasure houses, bridges — is rebuilt on adjacent plots in unhewn cypress with gold-tipped chigi and katsuogi ridge logs. The ritual rebuild copies the 7th-century state, making Ise a machine for transmitting pre-Buddhist architecture; carpenters train their careers against one rebuild.",
